description:
The while rx.try_recv().is_ok() {} loop silently discards presses. Add debug log to show how many were discarded for debugging.

## Implementation

**File:** src/main.rs
**Location:** Line 123 (the drain loop)

**Change:**
```rust
// FROM:
while rx.try_recv().is_ok() {}

// TO:
let mut discarded = 0;
while rx.try_recv().is_ok() {
    discarded += 1;
}
if discarded > 0 {
    log::debug!("Discarded {} extra button press(es)", discarded);
}
```

**Testing:**
```bash
RUST_LOG=debug cargo run
```
Rapidly press Enter multiple times, verify log shows discarded count.

Handle welcome on startup separately, then use simple loop: rx.recv() to block, drain extras, handle press. Eliminates confusing pending_press flag.

## Implementation

**File:** src/main.rs
**Location:** run_radio() function, lines 102-181

**Step 1:** Extract welcome logic to separate function:
```rust
fn handle_welcome(player: &mut audio::Player, tts: &std::sync::Arc<tts::PiperTts>) {
    info!("Welcome channel - checking for updates");
    player.speak_sync("Hello!", tts);
    player.speak_sync("Checking for updates. Please wait.", tts);
    // ... update check logic ...
    player.speak_sync("Press button to start radio.", tts);
}
```

**Step 2:** Simplify main loop:
```rust
// Handle welcome on startup (no button press needed)
handle_welcome(&mut player, &tts);

let mut channel_idx: usize = 0;  // 0 = first channel, after welcome

loop {
    // Wait for button press
    rx.recv().ok();
    while rx.try_recv().is_ok() {}  // Drain extras

    channel_idx += 1;
    let num_channels = channels::CHANNELS.len();

    if channel_idx > num_channels {
        // Off state
        player.stop();
        player.speak_sync("Radio off", &tts);
        channel_idx = 0;  // Reset, next press goes to welcome
        // Wait for next press to restart
        rx.recv().ok();
        while rx.try_recv().is_ok() {}
        handle_welcome(&mut player, &tts);
    } else {
        // Play channel
        let channel = &channels::CHANNELS[channel_idx - 1];
        player.speak(channel.tts_name, &tts);
        player.play_stream(channel.url)?;
    }
}
```

**Note:** This task pairs well with lego-radio-cq8 (enum state). Consider doing that one first.

**Testing:** cargo run, verify welcome plays on start, then channels cycle correctly

Current state uses magic numbers (-1, 0, 1-N, N+1). Replace with explicit enum: RadioState { Welcome, Playing(usize), Off }. This eliminates the -1 hack and makes transitions explicit.

## Implementation

**File:** src/main.rs

**Step 1:** Add enum at top of file (after imports):
```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]
enum RadioState {
    Welcome,
    Playing(usize),  // channel index 0-3
    Off,
}

impl RadioState {
    fn next(self, num_channels: usize) -> RadioState {
        match self {
            RadioState::Welcome => RadioState::Playing(0),
            RadioState::Playing(i) if i + 1 < num_channels => RadioState::Playing(i + 1),
            RadioState::Playing(_) => RadioState::Off,
            RadioState::Off => RadioState::Welcome,
        }
    }
}
```

**Step 2:** Replace state variables in run_radio():
```rust
// FROM:
let mut channel_idx: i32 = -1;
let mut pending_press = true;

// TO:
let mut state = RadioState::Welcome;
let mut first_run = true;
```

**Step 3:** Refactor main loop to use match on state:
```rust
loop {
    if !first_run {
        rx.recv().ok();
        while rx.try_recv().is_ok() {}
        state = state.next(channels::CHANNELS.len());
    }
    first_run = false;

    match state {
        RadioState::Welcome => { /* welcome logic */ }
        RadioState::Playing(idx) => { /* play channels::CHANNELS[idx] */ }
        RadioState::Off => { /* stop and say radio off */ }
    }
}
```

**Testing:** cargo test && cargo run, verify all state transitions work

